  "worked_solution": "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the student's math, science, and language test results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(70,{6}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(65,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(55,{15}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total score of a randomly selected student. So, we have\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(70+65+55) \\\\  & =190, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={6}^{2}+{10}^{2}+{15}^{2} \\\\  & =36+100+225 \\\\  & =361.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(190,361).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>200),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>200) & =P(Z>\\frac{200-190}{\\sqrt{361}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.53) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.53) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.53)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.53)≈0.7019.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>200) & ≈1-Φ(0.53) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7019 \\\\  & =0.2981.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
